\description{
Computes a confidence set for the ratio of two population means,
\eqn{\rho = \mu_x / \mu_y}, when both variables are measured on the same
units (the same participants before and after treatment, two
instruments applied to the same cases, two procedures scored on the same
items), or, with a third aligned variable, for the ratio of the two
variables' advantages over it, \eqn{\rho = (\mu_x - \mu_z) / (\mu_y -
\mu_z)}. The second form is the fraction of a reference procedure's
advantage over a trivial baseline that a candidate procedure retains,
the retention of effect of three-arm noninferiority designs (Pigeot et
al., 2003) when all three are evaluated on the same units. The default
interval is Fieller's, which inverts the one-sample \emph{t} test of the
per-unit contrast \eqn{x - \rho y} and is reported as what it is: a
bounded interval, the complement of an interval, or the whole line. When
the units are grouped (several items from each of a set of topics,
several patients from each clinic), \code{cluster} gives the
cluster-robust version. The log-scale interval of the response ratio and
percentile and BCa bootstrap intervals are offered beside it. For
independent groups, see \code{\link{ci_ratio_of_means}}.
}
\details{
\strong{Fieller's confidence set.} For a candidate ratio \eqn{\rho} the
per-unit contrast \eqn{d_i = x_i - \rho y_i} (with a baseline,
\eqn{(x_i - z_i) - \rho (y_i - z_i)}) has mean zero exactly when
\eqn{\rho} is the population ratio, so the one-sample \emph{t} test of
\eqn{d} on \eqn{N - 1} degrees of freedom tests each candidate, and the
confidence set is every \eqn{\rho} it does not reject (Fieller, 1941,
1954). The 1941 reference is Fieller's study of the biological
standardization of insulin, which Fieller (1954) cites with the year
1940. Written through the means and their covariance matrix \eqn{V},
the test statistic is
\deqn{T(\rho) = \frac{\hat\theta_1 - \rho \hat\theta_2}
  {\sqrt{v_{11} - 2 \rho v_{12} + \rho^2 v_{22}}},}{T(rho) =
  (theta1 - rho theta2) / sqrt(v11 - 2 rho v12 + rho^2 v22),}
with \eqn{V} the sample covariance matrix of the two variables divided
by \eqn{N}, and the set is the solution of a quadratic inequality in
\eqn{\rho}. With equal tail areas and no clusters, each finite end
\eqn{L} of the set (a limit or an end of the gap) is a value at which
\code{t.test(x - L * y)} has \emph{p}-value \code{1 - conf_level}
(with a baseline, \code{t.test((x - baseline) - L * (y - baseline))}),
and under bivariate normality the coverage is exact. The form of the
set is decided by the denominator's \emph{t},
\eqn{\hat\theta_2 / \sqrt{v_{22}}}, in the \code{t_denominator} row
(Fieller, 1954, p. 177). The row keeps the sign of the denominator, and
only its absolute value matters: when the absolute value of the
denominator's \emph{t} exceeds the critical value (with unequal tail
areas, each side of the estimate is bounded when that absolute value
exceeds the side's own critical value) the set is a bounded interval.
When it does not, the set contains arbitrarily large values of the
ratio, as it should when the data cannot distinguish the denominator from
zero: it is then either the complement of an interval, every value
outside a gap, or, when the data are consistent with every ratio at the
chosen level, the whole line. The \code{ci_lower} and
\code{ci_upper} rows are the outer ends of the set (\code{-Inf} and
\code{Inf} when it is unbounded), and the \code{excluded_lower} and
\code{excluded_upper} rows are the ends of the gap of a complement
(\code{NA} otherwise): the set is every value from \code{ci_lower} to
\code{ci_upper} except those strictly between \code{excluded_lower}
and \code{excluded_upper}. The \code{confidence_set} attribute names the
form, and so does the line printed under the table;
\code{\link{results_sentence}} writes the set in that form, for example
as every value except those between the ends of the gap. With unequal
tail areas one side of the set can be bounded while the other is not,
which can also give an interval unbounded on one side or the union of two
intervals. Fieller's own example in the examples below shows the three
forms from one data set, at three confidence levels. von Luxburg and
Franz (2009) derive the same sets geometrically, from confidence
intervals for the means of linear combinations of the two variables.

\strong{Clustered units.} When units come in clusters whose members are
correlated, the per-unit standard error understates the uncertainty.
With \code{cluster}, \eqn{V} is the cluster-robust covariance matrix
formed from the cluster sums of the centered variables,
\deqn{V = \frac{Q}{Q - 1} \frac{1}{N^2} \sum_{q = 1}^{Q} u_q u_q^\top,}{V
  = Q / (Q - 1) * sum(u_q u_q') / N^2,}
where \eqn{u_q} holds the sums over cluster \eqn{q} of the two variables'
deviations from their means; with \eqn{Q/(Q - 1)} as its small-sample
factor it is the variance of an intercept that \pkg{sandwich}'s
\code{vcovCL(type = "HC1")} gives. The reference distribution is
\emph{t} on \eqn{Q - 1} degrees of freedom, and the number of clusters is
the \code{n_clusters} row.

\strong{The log ratio.} \code{ci_method = "log"} forms the interval for
\eqn{\log \rho} from the delta method standard error,
\eqn{\sqrt{v_{11}/\hat\theta_1^2 + v_{22}/\hat\theta_2^2 -
2 v_{12}/(\hat\theta_1 \hat\theta_2)}}, on the same degrees of freedom,
and maps it back by the exponential. This is the response ratio of
meta-analysis (Hedges, Gurevitch, & Curtis, 1999) with the covariance
term that correlated means need (Lajeunesse, 2011). It is defined only
when both terms of the ratio are positive, and is refused otherwise with
a condition of class \code{dmar_log_ratio_undefined} (which inherits
\code{dmar_refusal}). It is always bounded; when the denominator is well
determined it is close to Fieller's interval, and the two part company as
the denominator's \emph{t} falls.

\strong{The bootstrap.} \code{"percentile"} and \code{"bca"} resample
units (pairs, or triples with a baseline), or whole clusters when
\code{cluster} is supplied, and compute the ratio in every replication
(Davison & Hinkley, 1997). The BCa interval reads its limits at
quantile positions moved from those of the percentile interval by a
bias correction, the median bias of the replications in normal units,
and by an acceleration, the rate at which the standard error of the
estimate changes with the parameter on a normalized scale (Efron &
Tibshirani, 1993); its acceleration is estimated by the jackknife,
deleting one unit (or one cluster) at a time. A bootstrap interval for
a ratio is always bounded, whatever the denominator's \emph{t}, so it
can undercover badly when the denominator is near zero, exactly where
Fieller's set becomes unbounded. When the Fieller set at the same level
is unbounded, the line under the table says so. Summary statistics
cannot be resampled, so a bootstrap from them is refused with a
condition of class
\code{dmar_bootstrap_needs_data}.

\strong{Bootstrap replications.} The limits are quantiles of the
\code{B} replicated ratios. A limit that cuts off a tail area
\eqn{\alpha} is located by the replications beyond it, so the percentile
interval is run only when \code{B} puts at least 10 replications beyond
each limit asked for (400 with 0.025 in each tail; with unequal tail
areas the smaller one decides, and a one-sided bound needs 10 beyond its
one limit), and the BCa interval only with 50 beyond each nominal limit
(2000 with 0.025 in each tail). A replication in which the ratio is
undefined (a denominator of exactly zero, which discrete data can
produce) is dropped and counted, never replaced, and the interval is
computed from the rest. The result records \code{B} (requested),
\code{B_used} (the number the interval rests on), \code{n_dropped}, and
\code{B_required} (the fewest replications a request may ask for; that
count is held to \code{B}, not to \code{B_used}) as attributes, and the
line under the table states them,
with \code{bootstrap_status} (\code{"complete"}, \code{"dropped"},
\code{"exploratory"}, \code{"insufficient_count"}, or
\code{"insufficient_share"}). One warning of class
\code{dmar_bootstrap_replications_dropped} states the counts when any
replication was dropped, with a caution when the dropped replications
are at least the 2.5 percent of \code{B} that lies beyond one limit at
the 95 percent level, because they could then decide by themselves where
that limit falls. When more than half were dropped, the limits asked for
are \code{NA}, the
estimate is returned, and the warning has class
\code{dmar_bootstrap_insufficient}. For the BCa interval the count of 10
is applied again where the bias correction and the acceleration place
the limits, and when fewer than 10 replications lie beyond one of them
no BCa interval is reported (limits \code{NA}, the same class, with the
two positions as \code{positions} and a suggested \code{B} as
\code{B_suggest}). A BCa interval whose bias correction is infinite
(every replicate on one side of the estimate) or whose jackknife is
undefined (deleting one observation leaves a denominator of zero) stops
with an error that names \code{ci_method = "percentile"}.
\code{options(dmar.bootstrap_exploratory = TRUE)} lets a smaller
\code{B} (2 or more) run for a first look or a demonstration; every such
call gives a warning of class \code{dmar_bootstrap_exploratory} and the
result is not one to report. See \code{\link{dmar_bootstrap}} for the
rule and its reasons.

\strong{Reading the interval for noninferiority.} With a baseline, the
candidate is shown to retain more than a fraction \eqn{f} of the
reference's advantage, at one-sided level \code{alpha_lower}, exactly
when \eqn{f} is below the lower limit of a set that is a bounded
interval (or, for a one-sided bound, an interval unbounded only above)
and whose denominator is positive. The one-sided bound is
\code{alpha_lower = 0.05, alpha_upper = 0}, and a two-sided 95 percent
interval gives the bound at one-sided level .025. Reporting the limit
lets each reader apply his or her own fraction; the function itself
tests no fraction and states no verdict. When the denominator is
negative, or the set is unbounded below, the lower limit does not carry
that reading.
}
\examples{
# Depressive symptoms at the end of treatment relative to the start,
# for the ten participants given the SSRI: the ratio of the posttest
# mean to the pretest mean of the Beck Depression Inventory.
ssri <- depression_bdi[depression_bdi$condition == "ssri", ]
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(ssri$bdi_post, ssri$bdi_pre)

# Fieller's (1954, p. 180) example, the ratio of the mean effects of two
# hypnotic drugs given to the same ten patients, from his summary
# statistics (variances of the means 0.3201 and 0.4009, covariance
# 0.2848). Its denominator's t is 3.68, so the set is a bounded interval
# at 95 percent, the complement of an interval at 99.5 percent, and the
# whole line at 99.9 percent, as Fieller reported.
drug_a_sd <- sqrt(10 * 0.3201)
drug_b_sd <- sqrt(10 * 0.4009)
r_ab <- 0.2848 / sqrt(0.3201 * 0.4009)
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(mean_1 = 0.75, sd_1 = drug_a_sd, mean_2 = 2.33,
                         sd_2 = drug_b_sd, r = r_ab, N = 10)
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(mean_1 = 0.75, sd_1 = drug_a_sd, mean_2 = 2.33,
                         sd_2 = drug_b_sd, r = r_ab, N = 10,
                         conf_level = 0.995)
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(mean_1 = 0.75, sd_1 = drug_a_sd, mean_2 = 2.33,
                         sd_2 = drug_b_sd, r = r_ab, N = 10,
                         conf_level = 0.999)

# A retained fraction: a candidate procedure, a reference procedure, and
# a trivial baseline scored on the same 300 items, which come from 60
# topics of five items each. The ratio is the share of the reference's
# gain over the baseline that the candidate keeps, with a one-sided
# 95 percent lower bound and topics as clusters.
set.seed(113)
topic <- rep(1:60, each = 5)
topic_effect <- rnorm(60, sd = 0.08)[topic]
trivial <- 0.20 + topic_effect + rnorm(300, sd = 0.10)
reference <- trivial + 0.15 + rnorm(300, sd = 0.10)
candidate <- trivial + 0.13 + rnorm(300, sd = 0.10)
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(candidate, reference, baseline = trivial,
                         cluster = topic, alpha_lower = 0.05,
                         alpha_upper = 0)

# A percentile bootstrap interval for the SSRI ratio; B = 2000 keeps the
# example quick, and a reported interval deserves the default B = 10000.
ci_ratio_of_means_paired(ssri$bdi_post, ssri$bdi_pre,
                         ci_method = "percentile", B = 2000, seed = 113)

}
